  hetu: three verdict organs read the same claim and the Pinian scheduler does not decide between them. This is not a tie to be broken: it is the fourth position, and the machine holds it open.
  nasta: the single verdict you asked for. It does not exist, and that is a fact about the three organs, not about this session.
  nasta: ORGAN A (1.1.1) NayaKosha/Obstruction reads: syad-avaktavyam
  nasta: bhanga : Position B4Avaktavya
  nasta: samata : satya (truth value only)
  nasta: NOT IDENTIFIABLE. They agree in TRUTH VALUE and differ in CONTENT.
  nasta: This is Durnaya_CollapseIffEveryNayaAgrees exactly: collapse is
  nasta: available iff every pair of fibres is equivalent, and `Mixed` with
  nasta: fibres Unit and Bool is the checked witness that both-inhabited is
  nasta: not enough. Any single verdict here discards the difference below.
  nasta: maximal collapsible sub-families (varga), by content: { #0 } { #1 }
  nasta: WHAT A SINGLE VERDICT WOULD DESTROY:
  nasta: from `episode-is-real (#1)`, 2:
  nasta: - the diagnosis at 39
  nasta: - the documented 2025 episode
  nasta: from `harm-is-real (#0)`, 2:
  nasta: - the Oct 2022 statements
  nasta: - the fear reported afterwards
  nasta: ORGAN B (1.1.2) Saptabhangi reads: syad-asti
  nasta: harm-is-real ‚í syad-asti
  nasta: episode-is-real ‚í syad-asti
  nasta: folded with sahaS ‚í syad-asti
  nasta: NOTE what this organ holds that the others do not: `syad-asti` is a PROFILE. After jihvbheda the fourth position does not record which two seeds produced it, and that module states this is the doctrine's claim, not a modelling artefact ‚î which is exactly why its saha does not associate.
  nasta: ORGAN C (1.1.3) SaptabhangiGarbha reads: syad-asti syad-asti
  nasta: sadhaka: harm-is-real <- the Oct 2022 statements [the statements themselves, dated]
  nasta: NOTE what this organ holds that the others do not: the fourth position here CARRIES both nayas and both witnesses (Sesa), and `caturthatTritiya` reads them back out. Where the profile organ says the seeds are consumed, this one says they are retained.
  nasta: DEFECT -- vipratiedha undecided at site 0.
  nasta: Two or more rules contend and no metarule decides. The scheduler does
  nasta: NOT choose: breaking this tie by list position would be a silent
  nasta: collapse (AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA ¬ß‡: transport or a written defect, no
  nasta: third path). A caller may fall back to prva -- the weakest of the
  nasta: five paribhs -- to preserve an existing engine's behaviour, but only
  nasta: after this record exists, and it is then prva that is reported, not a decision. candidates:
  nasta: 1.1.1 NayaKosha/Obstruction ‚î B1..B7 + ADharmin -- NayaKosha/Obstruction ‚î B1..B7 + ADharmin
  nasta: 1.1.2 Saptabhangi ‚î presence profiles + Apratipatti -- Saptabhangi ‚î presence profiles + Apratipatti
  nasta: 1.1.3 SaptabhangiGarbha ‚î proof-relevant, residue carries both nayas -- SaptabhangiGarbha ‚î proof-relevant, residue carries both nayas
  nasta: metarules tried, in Paribhenduekhara 38 order:
  nasta: Apavada no candidate is an apavda to all the others
  nasta: Antaranga the domain abstains: no nimitta depth is defined for these offers
  nasta: Nitya no candidate applies-after-the-other while the others do not (ktktaprasaga is mutual or empty)
  nasta: Para the domain declares its rule order unauthored: a list index is not a position, so 1.4.2 has nothing to compare
  nasta: EA RETAINED BY THE SCHEDULER ‚î the object contended over: the claim
  nasta: offer 1.1.1 at site 0, width 1, result `syad-avaktavyam` ‚î NayaKosha/Obstruction ‚î B1..B7 + ADharmin
  nasta: offer 1.1.2 at site 0, width 1, result `syad-asti` ‚î Saptabhangi ‚î presence profiles + Apratipatti
  nasta: offer 1.1.3 at site 0, width 1, result `syad-asti` ‚î SaptabhangiGarbha ‚î proof-relevant, residue carries both nayas
  sesa: A ‚â B IS available and is exhibited by saptabhangi.samkramana, checked exhaustively over all sixteen round trips ‚î those two may be identified and this machine identifies them
  sesa: C is NOT equivalent to either, in either direction, and saptabhangi.nasti computes the collision
  sesa: so the residue is exactly one distinction: whether the fourth position retains the two seeds that produced it. B says it does not, and calls that the doctrine. C says it does, and reads them back out with caturthatTritiya. That is the next question, and it is a question about Akalaka, not about Haskell.
  sesa: AHIMSA_SUTRA_VISTARA ¬ß7: ‡®‡Ø‡‡‡¶‡ ‡‡ô‡‡ï‡‡‡‡‡ã ‡® ‡µ‡ø‡¶‡‡Ø‡‡ ‡ ‡® ‡µ‡∞‡‡‡ø‡‡ ‡ ‡® ‡‡®‡‡‡ø‡‡ ‡ ‡® ‡‡‡ø‡‡‡ü‡ ‡ ‡® ‡µ‡ø‡¶‡‡Ø‡‡ ‡
  sesa: and a datum for that question, arriving from a third direction while this was being wired: the scheduler's own `Avaktavya` carried only a rendering (`Dosa`) when this machine first read it, and now carries a `Sesa` holding the contending offers ENTIRE ‚î the scheduler lane reached `the fourth position must retain what produced it` independently, for its own reasons, and cites SaptabhangiGarbha's header in doing so. Two lanes arriving at C's position is not a proof of it; it is evidence about where to look, and it is recorded here rather than left in a diff.
